  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 220 PM CDT Tue Jul 21 2026\n\nToday - Tonight:\n\nA secondary cold front is moving southward through the Northland\nthis afternoon with weak cyclonic flow behind it. There is just\nenough low-level moisture in this pattern to produce\nintermittent sprinkles and a couple very light rain showers this\nafternoon in portions of northeast Minnesota and northwest\nWisconsin, but very little to no rain accumulations are\nexpected. Otherwise, partly to mostly cloudy skies continue\nthrough early evening alongside northerly to north-northwesterly\nwinds gusting to 20-30 mph and substantially cooler high \ntemperatures in the mid-60s north to mid-70s south relative to\nwhat we have been seeing recently. Despite relatively higher \nminimum relative humidity values of 40-50% this afternoon, the \nstronger winds set the stage for near-critical fire weather \nconditions in much of northeastern Minnesota through early \nevening.\n\nMuch more comfortable low temperatures in the mid-40s to around\n50 degrees settle in tonight alongside calm to light and \nvariable winds and clear to mostly clear skies.\n\nWednesday - Early Thursday:\n\nHigh pressure sliding through the region on Wednesday into early\nThursday will keep the weather during this timeframe dry with\nmild conditions continuing. Some scattered diurnal cumulus\ndevelops mid/late Wednesday morning into early evening, \notherwise conditions will be mostly clear. High temperatures on\nWednesday will be seasonal in the low to upper 70s, with\nWednesday night lows in the upper 40s to mid-50s.\n\nLate this Week - Weekend: \n\nThe upper-level pattern becomes amplified late this week into\nweekend with broad mid- and upper-level ridging over the western\nU.S. and a trough over eastern Canada/eastern U.S. This will set\nup northwest flow over the Upper Midwest in which a few\nshortwave troughs could move through the region while\ntemperature trends will be on the rise. The first round of\nprecipitation potential would be a 10-40% chance for in far \nnorthern Minnesota--mainly north of the Iron Range--late\nThursday and Thursday evening. The better chances (30-80%,\nhighest across northern Minnesota) would be with another\nshortwave trough and attendant surface front Friday evening into\nSaturday morning. There are additional, low-end shower and storm chances\n(20-40% chances) on Saturday and Sunday, but confidence is \nlower as to the timing and southward extent of any shortwave\ntroughs rounding the top of the ridge into the Northland due to\nthe closer proximity of the mid/upper-level ridge axis by that\ntime. Depending on how quickly the cold front clears the\nNorthland on Saturday, there could be a brief window of time\nlate Saturday where an instability/shear combination could \nsupport strong to severe storms. There is also a weak signal for\nstrong to severe storm potential on Sunday, but confidence in\npotential and locations is low at this time. Regardless, there\nis not a strong signal for widespread precipitation amounts over\nthe next 7 days, with probabilities of >0.25\" through the\nweekend ranging from 50-60% in the Iron Range and north to\n30-40% in much of northeast Minnesota and northwest Wisconsin\nand 20% in central Minnesota.\n\nHigh temperatures gradually warm from the low to mid 80s on\nThursday to mid-80s to low 90s on Saturday and Sunday, though\nonshore flow/lake breeze on Sunday would likely keep the hottest\nconditions away from Lake Superior. Still, this heat would\nlikely lead to Heat Advisory for portions of the Northland this\nweekend. These hotter conditions combined with fairly low \nrelative humidity and breezy conditions at times could lead to a\nreturn of elevated fire conditions for Friday into this weekend.",
